WHY THIS FILE SAYS "ALL RIGHTS RESERVED" AND NOT SOMETHING FRIENDLIER

Because the intention is to sell this, and the two directions are not
symmetrical. Publishing under an open licence is irrevocable in practice: every
copy taken while it was open stays open, forever, and no later decision brings
it back. Starting closed forecloses nothing — a licence can always be loosened
afterwards, and dual-licensing remains available. So the reversible option is
the one to start from, and it stays right until a deliberate decision replaces
it.

This covers the code in this repository ONLY. It says nothing about what the
software PRODUCES, and nothing about the third-party models, services, fonts
and stock sources the pipeline draws on — those carry their own terms, several
of which distinguish commercial from non-commercial use in ways that a
monetised channel runs straight into.

Those are tracked separately and checkably:

    python scripts/licensing.py

That command reads the licences of the installed dependencies from their own
metadata, works out which models and services the CURRENT configuration
actually switches on, and names every one whose terms nobody has read yet. It
is not legal advice; it is the list of questions, kept honest, with the page
that answers each.

THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

Dependencies are listed in requirements.txt and requirements-optional.txt and
retain their own licences, including at least one copyleft licence in the core
set. Bundled assets — currently the Anton typeface under assets/fonts — retain
theirs. Redistributing this software in any form means carrying those notices
with it.
